def build(course, m):
    return course(93, 'Coastal Climate Risk and Inclusive Adaptation',
        'Coastal Climate Risk\nand Inclusive\nAdaptation',
        'Explain a coastal risk pathway and assess adaptation options for service continuity, unequal impacts and feasibility.', [
        m('Trace compound coastal risk',
            'Separate hazard, exposure, vulnerability and capacity in a coastal case.',
            """
A hazard is a potential physical event or trend, such as recurrent inundation. Exposure concerns the people, infrastructure and activities in its path. Vulnerability concerns the conditions that make harm more likely or more serious, such as insecure access or limited ways to replace lost income. Capacity includes resources people can use, such as a staffed alternate route. The same event can have different consequences. [S82]

Make a time sequence that links the physical event with changes in work and services. In a fictional village, flooding blocks the footpath to a market, power failure interrupts ice production and a missed selling window reduces earnings. The risks can compound. Ask which link is supported by an observation and where the explanation needs further evidence. [S82]

Take local observations seriously and record their date, location and conditions. If traders report floodwater arriving earlier, ask what period they mean and what else has changed. A claim about a particular storm's causes needs separate evidence. Findings from purposively selected Lagos respondents can motivate questions about warnings and preservation. Check the barriers and resources in the fictional setting itself. [R92]

Draw a simple risk chain with a marked point for potential intervention. A second contact route may reduce exposure to service disruption while leaving the flood hazard unchanged. An accessible relocation option may reduce travel difficulties for some and create costs for others. Identify one change that could weaken a proposed intervention and who can monitor it after a test. [S82] Check the path at more than one time of day in the fictional record so a one-off observation does not drive the whole decision.
            """,
            'Floodwater closes the footpath to the landing market. Power fails during the same period, and fish handlers miss the buying window.',
            'Label the hazard, exposure, two vulnerability factors and one capacity. Trace a cascade into income or service access.',
            'Map flooding and power loss, who relies on the route, and the ways delays translate into spoilage or lost earnings.',
            """
Define the hazard.|What is the physical hazard in the supplied market case?|Floodwater reaching the market path.|The lack of alternative earnings.|The need for a private interview.|The dependence on one buyer.|Hazard describes the potentially damaging event.
Identify exposure.|Which entry identifies exposure?|A market and access path located in the inundation area.|A household has little savings.|A staff member has an alternate route.|A person understands warnings.|Exposure follows presence in the hazard area.
Separate vulnerability from capacity.|What is a vulnerability factor during a market closure?|Few feasible alternative ways to earn or sell.|A staffed fallback service exists.|A routine review is scheduled.|A functioning second landing has capacity.|Vulnerability increases the consequences of disruption.
Map the cascade.|Which sequence explains a connected risk?|Power fails, ice is unavailable, and saleable stock can decline.|Income falls, therefore a flood must occur.|A notice is printed, therefore all losses stop.|One case report establishes the regional cause.|A cascade connects an event to successive practical impacts.
Bound the local claim.|What does a dated account about a blocked path establish most directly?|A reported local observation requiring context and verification.|Global attribution for the event.|A permanent forecast for every nearby shore.|A verified technical cause without other evidence.|Local observations have a defined place and scope.
            """),
        m('Design warnings people can act on',
            'Specify an accessible alert, an action route and a drill check.',
            """
A warning system needs a trusted source, an understandable message and a feasible action. A message reaching every registered handset proves little if many workers share devices, use different languages or lack a safe route. A learner exercise can use the responsible authority's supplied example alert without issuing or editing a real public warning. [S82]

Map who is likely to be at home, at the landing, at a market or travelling. Consider language, hearing, vision, literacy, connection and access to a trusted messenger. Pair communication channels such as voice and written information where feasible. Check if the message arrives with enough time to act and gives a contact route people can use. [R92]

Test the response with a fictional household that needs assistance to reach an agreed service point. Identify who provides support, who receives them and when the case is escalated. Include a privacy boundary for any contact list. An effective drill can check comprehension and responsibilities without broadcasting private details about someone needing help. Use official instructions and local emergency arrangements in real incidents. [S82]

Judge success using more than a send count. In twenty fictional cases, ten receive the alert, eight understand the instruction and six can complete the expected action. Keep the stages visible and identify why each group falls away. A trial might show a route needs repair or an action needs different timing. Assign a role and date to verify the corrected step. [R92] Try the drill with someone who missed the first message and someone who received it but could not complete the proposed action.
            """,
            'An alert is sent by text to 20 workers. Ten receive it, eight understand it and six can act. Some people need help moving to an agreed point.',
            'Design a second channel and an assistance handoff. Draft three stage-specific drill measures.',
            'Separate delivery, comprehension and successful action. Verify the alternative route and confirm who provides assistance.',
            """
Connect alert and action.|What must a usable warning convey in addition to the stated hazard?|What to do and how to access help within the available time.|Only the date of the previous meeting.|Only a registration number.|Only a sponsor slogan.|Information supports safety when people know an achievable action.
Design for different channels.|What helps people with unreliable mobile data receive a warning?|A complementary authorised channel that works in their context.|A larger photo attachment sent online only.|A test limited to the newest device.|A URL shared after the event.|Channel choice should reflect practical access.
Check assistance ownership.|Which drill result demonstrates a handoff?|A named helper and receiving role confirm coordinated support.|The organiser adds a new contact field.|The message is forwarded repeatedly.|A worker guesses that help is available.|An accountable handoff confirms responsibility.
Measure ability to act.|What fraction of the 20 exercise cases completed the expected action?|6 of 20.|8 of 20.|10 of 20.|20 of 20.|Six were able to act, a separate stage from understanding.
Protect contact details.|Which approach fits the assistance exercise?|Use minimal fictional details and confirm the authorised privately held route.|Publish each household's needs to an open list.|Send all identifiers through every channel.|Maintain an unrestricted register indefinitely.|Support details need a bounded operational purpose.
            """),
        m('Compare adaptations and displaced risk',
            'Appraise how an adaptation option distributes benefits, burdens and residual risk.',
            """
Define whose risk a proposed measure is meant to reduce. A drainage change may help a market but send water toward a settlement. A move to a higher site may reduce direct exposure while increasing transport costs, reducing access to customers or separating workers from care responsibilities. These consequences affect whether the option is feasible and equitable. [S82]

Distinguish short-term coping from sustained changes in risk. Borrowing to replace stock may cover one interruption and increase debt exposure when outages recur. Investing in a functioning alternative access route may reduce repeated disruption if maintenance and staffing are supported. Examine the conditions that must hold over time and their costs for different people. [S82]

A study of Nigerian fishing communities reports different adaptation choices and associations with cooperative participation. Study design and model assumptions matter. Use the finding to ask which local groups could access cooperative help, what terms apply and whether alternatives remain available. Avoid treating a count of adaptations as proof that risk decreased or participation was fair. [R93]

Compare an immediate low-cost response, an option requiring investment and the current route against shared criteria. Include access, maintenance, uncertainty, habitat impact and risk transferred to others. Identify a signal that should lead to a pause, such as observed obstruction of an established route. Decide who can review the signal and when. If physical works are proposed, identify the competent authority and assessment needed. [S82] Make the comparison explicit about duration. A one-season gain may require a different choice if operation adds costs each year or narrows future options.
            """,
            'An elevated stall area reduces flood exposure but adds a fare and a long walk. A drainage proposal could increase water near homes.',
            'Compare both options and the current route. List one evidence gap, a displaced risk and a trigger to revisit the recommendation.',
            'Balance safety gains with travel and distribution effects. The drainage claim needs qualified site review.',
            """
Identify whose risk changes.|Which drawback can arise from moving a market uphill?|Higher travel cost or reduced access for some users.|All users automatically gain identical safety.|Training costs necessarily disappear.|Protection from every hazard is assured.|A change can reduce one risk while creating another.
Distinguish coping and adaptation.|Which example is mainly short-term coping?|Borrowing to replace a single load after disruption.|Maintaining a verified alternate route over many seasons.|Revising a standing continuity procedure.|Providing a durable staffed fallback.|Coping addresses the immediate shock.
Examine displaced risk.|What finding should trigger reassessment of the drainage option?|Water may be redirected toward occupied homes.|The plan is printed in colour.|The promoter attends a meeting.|The map has a new title.|Adaptation can worsen risk elsewhere.
Use membership evidence carefully.|What question follows from the cooperative study?|Which groups can meet membership conditions and access help locally?|Can every association guarantee lower risk?|Should all nonmembers be excluded?|Can cross-sectional models prove every local outcome?|The evidence informs mechanisms and assumptions.
Define an observable review trigger.|Which trigger is usable for a fictional pathway decision?|Observe persistent loss of foot access at the agreed unloading window.|Wait until someone feels dissatisfied in general.|Let a sponsor change the test after approval.|Count all changes as improvements.|A trigger specifies a condition and supports an accountable response.
            """),
    ])
